    "millsfield:images_texts": [
      "SAS\nHow Great Thou Art\nSTEREO\nRECORDING\nUAS 822-52800 A/B\nSAS\nANDANANIAN\nGanddal Pikekor",
      "GANDDAL PIKEKOR\nAudun Vagle, Director\nrorsal Audio Corp.\n2541 Nicollet A SS40\nSIDE 1\n33½ RPM\n1. How Great Thou Art\n2. Goin' Home\n3. Hallelujakorot av \"Messias\"\n4. Den store hvite flokk\nSTEREO\nUAS.622-52800 A\n1366\n2009.161.005",
      "GANDDAL PIKEKOR\nAudun Vagle, Director\nUniversal Audio Con\nRECORDING\n1. Rondo\n2. Pal på haugen\n3. Småsporven\n4. Per Spolmann\n5. Kjerringa med staven\n6. Toccata from symphony no. 5\nSTEREO\nUAS.622-52800 B\n33½ RPM",
      "SAS\nHow Great Thou Art\nSTEREO\nRECORDING\nUAS 622-52800 A/B\nAN DINAVIAN\nSAS\n55-000\nGanddal Pikekor",
      "Ganddal Pikekor INTERNATIONALLY FAMOUS GIRLS CHOIR\nAudun Vagle (back), the 33 year old director and founder of\nthe Pikekor, is a high school music instructor and the organist\nin sha Hauland Church in Sandnes.\nHe has gained fame for his\nwork with choirs in Norway and abroad. Three of his six choirs\nwere among the ten finalists in a Norwegian national music\ncontest\nMons Leidvin Takle (front), organist, took his examinations\nwhen only 16 years old at the Stavanger Music Conservatory in\n1958\nStockholm and Copenhagen.\nLa te len a concert nianist and\nmade his debut in Oslo in 1966. He has since won much acclaim\nfor hie artistry\nThe Ganddal Pikekor (pronounced Pee'-keh-kor -\nTranslates:\nGirls Choir) is a community choir since\nit is made up of girls from not only the village of\nGandal but from surrounding Sandnes Township as\nwell.\nOriginally the Pikekor consisted of less than 20\nschool girls around twelve years of age.\nToday there\nare six choirs in all totaling over 350 girls.\nFrom this\nlarge group the best singers are chosen, forming the\nsmaller choir pictured on the album cover.\nMost of\nthese girls are about 18 to 20 years of age and have\nbeen singing in the Pikekor for as many as eight years.\nSide 1\n1. How Great Thou Art ...\nSwedish Folk Melody\nTekst: Carl Boberg.\nOversatt: Stuart K. Hine\nGoin' home.\nAnton Dvorak\n\"From the New World.\"\nOp. 95\nOversatt av William Arms\nFisher\nHallelujakoret av\n\"Messias\"\n... G. F. Händel\nDen store hvite flokk\nNorsk folketone\nBrorson\nSolist\nSide 2\n1. Rondo\nThe Pikekor tours Norway annually. They have\nmade frequent visits to Denmark,\n-in and\nSweden,\nEngland and much of the European continent.\nThey\ntoured the United States in 1968 where they were pop-\nularly acclaimed and eagerly received in concert halls\nand churches throughout the midwest. Their costumes\nare a delight to the eye and their singing most pleasant\ntone para\nTheir program is varied and versatile con-\nsisting of sacred songs as well as the cherished Scand-\ninavian folk songs and Gospel hymns.\n3.\nSmasporven\nNorsk folketone\narr. Fliflet Bræin\n4. Per spelmann\nDet står ein fria\nA vesle Kari var\nharel tallotond\narr. Fliflet Brain\nSolo: Berit Hetland\nArr. Daq Kristoffersen\nNorsk folketone\nKjerringa med staven.\nRim\nPer Hjort Albertsen\nNorsk folketone\narr. Thomas Beck\nNorsk folketone\n2. Pảt på haugen\nMons Leidvin Takle\nOrgel: Mons Leidvin Takle\nNorsk folkevise\narr. Fliflet Brain\nToccata from symphony\nnr. 5.\nCh. M. Widor\nOrgel: Mons Leidvin Takle\nLA= STEREO\nPRODUCED BY UA RECORDING - 2541 NICOLLET AVENUE, MINNEAPOLIS, MINNESOTA"
    ],
